# Lantern: a pixel art adventure in a new world every game, for AerOS
# Run it with:
#     lantern
#
# Controls:
#     Arrows or W A S D   - walk
#     Space or J          - swing your sword (it cuts bushes too)
#     E or Enter          - talk, open, take, pray, read (whatever is in front of you)
#     Q                   - drink a potion
#     Tab or I            - your journal: what you carry, what you know, what has happened
#     M                   - the map of the vale
#     Escape              - pause: save, or quit (you can quit at any time)
#
# THE GAME
#
#   You are the lamplighter of a village in a valley, and something has come to threaten it.
#   Each game is a new valley, worked out from a "seed" (a number, shown on the title, in the
#   journal and at the end): the land, the villages, the people and their names, the villain,
#   what they want and why, the relic that can stop them and where it lies. The same seed
#   always makes the same valley, so a good one can be played again ("Choose a seed").
#
#   A run is 15 to 30 minutes: find out from the one who asks for help what is wrong, find the
#   sage who knows where the relic is, fetch it from a cave, and take it to the villain's lair.
#   What you do on the way matters:
#
#   - Time passes. Each day is about four and a half minutes; at night it is dark but for your
#     lantern, the fires and the windows, and the monsters are bolder.
#   - From the second night the villain raids your village. Be there and fight, and it stands;
#     be away, and a house burns and someone is taken. Lose three times and it is razed.
#   - Someone is lost in the wild. Save them in time, or they are found too late.
#   - A traveller on the road asks for a few coins. Crates in the village belong to people:
#     you can take what is in them, but you may be seen. The shrine's offering bowl is full.
#   - The villain was not always a villain. The sage, or the traveller, can tell you why, and
#     what was lost; if you find it, you can choose not to fight.
#
#   There are five endings, depending on what you did and what you choose at the end.
#
# HOW IT WORKS, for anyone who wants to build on it
#
#   - The picture is a low resolution one (256 x 192 "pixels" on a 1024 x 768 screen) blown
#     up SCALE times. Every tile and sprite is a 16 x 16 picture in a "sheet" (a row of them in
#     one BMP), drawn with imgcol(): imgcol draws a range of a picture's columns stretched over
#     a box, so picture n of a sheet is its columns n * 16 to n * 16 + 16. It is all done in C,
#     a whole tile in one call, with a light level (1 bright, 0 black) and, for sprites, the
#     see-through parts left out.
#   - Light: each sheet comes in four lights (day, golden, dusk and a blue night). By day
#     everything is drawn from the day sheet. In the dark each tile is lit by whichever is
#     brightest: the darkness, your lantern (worked out from how far the tile is from you), or
#     the "light map" of fires, torches and windows worked out when the map was made. Tiles lit
#     by a flame are drawn from the golden sheet, the rest from the blue night one, so lamplight
#     looks warm and the dark looks cold.
#   - The world is made from the seed with a random number generator written here (so it is
#     the same on every computer): "value noise" for height and wetness gives water, sand,
#     grass, forest, swamp and mountains; the villages, ruins, cave, shrine and lair are
#     stamped on; then roads are found between them with Dijkstra's shortest path (cheap over
#     grass, dear through forest, rock and water), and paved, with bridges over water. Every
#     place the story needs is joined to the village by a road, so a world is always playable.
#   - The caves are rooms joined by corridors, each room to the next, so they are always
#     joined up too.
#   - The story is made from parts: four villains, each with a deed, a weakness, a lair, a
#     past and a lost one, names made from syllables, and who in the village asks for help.
#   - The pictures are drawn by tools/make_lantern_art.py and the music and sounds made by
#     tools/make_lantern_sounds.py; they are loaded from the game's folder, /apps/lantern/.
#
#   For testing: "lantern seed 1234" starts a new game in that world straight away, "lantern
#   world 1234" only makes the world and prints what it made, and "lantern demo 1234" plays a
#   game by itself to an ending (add "mercy" or "join" for those endings, and "fast" for days a
#   minute long), printing what it does. The game is saved in /apps/lantern/save.txt.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
